--TEST--
Core\Password::needsRehash names both sides of its cost floor on each axis — the configured cost itself is current and one unit below it is stale, on memory and on passes alike, with the floor read off a hash this class just wrote — spec § 16, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *bound on both sides* shape. `needsRehash` asks whether a stored hash
// has fallen behind, which is a comparison against two numbers, and a member
// that answered `false` for everything reads as correct against every
// "stronger is left alone" line while a member that answered `true` for
// everything reads as correct against every "weaker is stale" one.
// `password-needs-rehash-answers-weaker-not-different.nvst` asks both halves
// but never adjacently: its stale hash is 11 MiB below the floor and its
// current one 45 MiB above, so a comparison off by one unit — a `<=` where the
// module writes `<` — passes it in either direction. Here each side of each
// axis is one unit from the other.
//
// The floor is not written down twice. The literals below are the parameters
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/password.rs` picked, and the last block reads them
// back off a hash this class has just made, so a parameter change that left
// this case behind fails on that line rather than turning the five above it
// into assertions about a number nothing uses any more.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";

// One salt and one digest throughout: `needsRehash` reads the parameters off
// the PHC string and never recomputes the hash, so only the header varies.
string $atFloor = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
string $oneKibUnder = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19455,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
string $oneKibOver = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19457,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
string $onePassUnder = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=1,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
string $onePassOver = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=3,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';

// The floor is a floor and not a target: the configured cost is current, and
// the first value below it is not. Named adjacently, one KiB apart.
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($atFloor)) {
    echo "the configured cost asked to rehash itself\n";
} else {
    echo "the configured memory cost is current\n";
}
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($oneKibUnder)) {
    echo "one KiB under is stale\n";
} else {
    echo "one KiB under was reported current\n";
}
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($oneKibOver)) {
    echo "one KiB over was asked to downgrade itself\n";
} else {
    echo "one KiB over is current\n";
}

// The same bound on the other axis, where a pass is the unit. Memory is held
// at the floor in both, so each answer is about `t` alone.
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($onePassUnder)) {
    echo "one pass under is stale\n";
} else {
    echo "one pass under was reported current\n";
}
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($onePassOver)) {
    echo "one pass over was asked to downgrade itself\n";
} else {
    echo "one pass over is current\n";
}

// And the floor the five lines above are written against is the one this class
// actually writes, rather than a number that was true when the case was
// authored. A hash made now sits exactly at it.
string $stored = Core\Password::hash($password);
if (Core\Str::startsWith($stored, '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$')) {
    echo "and a hash made now sits on that floor\n";
} else {
    echo $stored, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
the configured memory cost is current
one KiB under is stale
one KiB over is current
one pass under is stale
one pass over is current
and a hash made now sits on that floor
